\subsection{\code{CoxDeviance$sample_score()}}{
  Draws of the score at a linear predictor: from the Cox
model with that linear predictor, or (\code{"wild"}) from its score
residuals.

Each draw \code{r} has one entry per observation, on the scale of the
score \code{-gradient / 2}, so that \code{crossprod(X, r)} is a draw of the
score of the coefficients of a design \code{X}. The model-based methods
draw the failures at each event time from the model and return their
score: the sum over the failures of \code{x_j} minus its expectation, with
risk weights \code{sample_weight * exp(linear_predictor)}. They differ
only at tied event times, and need equal positive weights.
\itemize{
\item \code{"multinomial"}: the \code{d} failures drawn with replacement, with
probabilities proportional to the risk weights: the sampling model
of Breslow's likelihood. Its covariance is the Breslow information.
\item \code{"bernoulli"}: each subject at risk fails independently with
probability \verb{min(1, d p)}, a Cox-model analogue of the weird
bootstrap, centered with the number of failures drawn. Its mean is
zero only while \verb{d p < 1}.
\item \code{"fisher"}: the set of \code{d} failures drawn from Fisher's noncentral
hypergeometric distribution, the sampling model of the exact
discrete partial likelihood, centered at its exact mean. Its
covariance is the information of the exact likelihood.
\item \code{"wild"}: the score residuals (of the likelihood set by
\code{tie_breaking}) times independent standard normals. Its covariance
is the sandwich, which does not need the model to be right.
}

See the package README for references.
